WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:05.029
When Americans turned on their TVs in the
early 1990s, one contentious issue was hard

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:07.120
to miss: immigration.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.804
Is immigration good for America?

00:00:08.804 --> 00:00:11.145
The federal government won't stop them at
the border.

00:00:11.145 --> 00:00:14.508
You spend $5.5 billion a year to support them.

00:00:14.508 --> 00:00:17.676
There’s a right way. And there’s a wrong way.

00:00:17.676 --> 00:00:22.900
At the time, there were around 5 million undocumented
immigrants in the US.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.413
And most Americans saw immigrants as a burden on the country,

00:00:26.413 --> 00:00:28.915
taking jobs, housing and healthcare,

00:00:28.915 --> 00:00:32.198
and thought immigration as a whole should be decreased.

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:37.035
Our country is invaded by immigrants who
are like cancer cells.

00:00:37.035 --> 00:00:40.498
That same year, Republicans ran on a tough-on-immigration
platform

00:00:40.498 --> 00:00:42.573
and took control of Congress.

00:00:42.573 --> 00:00:46.400
Democrats were pushed to adopt tough positions
on immigration, too.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.610
We are a nation of immigrants.

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:51.260
But we are also a nation of laws.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.530
In 1996, President Bill Clinton signed a major
piece of legislation:

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:59.850
the Illegal Immigration Reform and Immigrant
Responsibility Act,

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:00.899
or, IIRIRA.

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:05.537
It’s goal was to decrease the number of
undocumented immigrants.

00:01:05.537 --> 00:01:07.811
It did the opposite.

00:01:11.557 --> 00:01:16.307
Before the 1990s, undocumented immigration
into the US looked very different.

00:01:16.307 --> 00:01:18.686
For one, it was usually temporary.

00:01:18.686 --> 00:01:21.150
People used to go back and forth across the
border.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.909
They would go north for the harvest, and they
would earn some money,

00:01:23.909 --> 00:01:25.945
and they would go back to Mexico.

00:01:25.945 --> 00:01:28.575
And if they wanted to come live permanently
in the US,

00:01:28.575 --> 00:01:31.365
there were a few legal channels, but not many.

00:01:31.365 --> 00:01:35.200
If they married an American citizen, they
could get lawful status.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.608
Or if maybe their brother was a citizen already,
he could sponsor them.

00:01:38.608 --> 00:01:40.529
Or an employer could.

00:01:40.529 --> 00:01:46.186
And these could be done after they were already
living in the US undocumented.

00:01:46.186 --> 00:01:50.506
Before 1996, the threat of deportation was
relatively low.

00:01:50.506 --> 00:01:53.447
People were commonly deported for committing
a crime.

00:01:53.447 --> 00:01:57.832
And it was usually limited to major crimes
— like murder or trafficking.

00:01:57.832 --> 00:02:03.439
But IIRIRA, together with other 1996 laws,
drastically expanded deportable crimes

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:06.892
to even minor infractions, like shoplifting.

00:02:06.892 --> 00:02:09.582
It was also retroactive.

00:02:09.582 --> 00:02:13.999
So say it’s 1976, and someone is caught
stealing some albums from the mall —

00:02:13.999 --> 00:02:15.730
they wouldn’t be deported.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:19.120
Over the next 20 years, they never commit
another crime.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:24.659
But after 1996, they could be deported because
of that old misdemeanor.

00:02:24.659 --> 00:02:27.090
And not just if they were currently undocumented:

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:30.473
this applied to immigrants with lawful status,
too.

00:02:30.473 --> 00:02:35.331
And previously an immigration judge could
decide if the deportation should even take place.

00:02:35.331 --> 00:02:37.840
Now things were a little more automatic.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.620
Ignoring the fact that those deportations
would be extremely

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:45.334
harmful to US citizen children or spouses.

00:02:45.334 --> 00:02:47.521
Deportations skyrocketed.

00:02:47.521 --> 00:02:51.809
And IIRIRA created the framework for future
laws that further expanded

00:02:51.809 --> 00:02:55.584
reasons people could be deported, especially
after 9/11.

00:02:55.584 --> 00:03:01.361
But IIRAIRA also made another huge, fundamental
change in the US immigration system.

00:03:01.361 --> 00:03:06.680
One of the aspects of the 1996 law that is
particularly strict and I think

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:11.392
in many respects, inhumane, is the so-called
3 and 10 year bars.

00:03:11.392 --> 00:03:16.409
Those 3 and 10 year bars made these legal
pathways nearly impossible to obtain.

00:03:16.409 --> 00:03:18.000
They work like this:

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.109
Anyone who’s been undocumented in the US
for 6 months

00:03:21.109 --> 00:03:24.430
and wants to gain legal status, first has
to leave the country

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:28.220
and be barred from returning for 3 years.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:32.989
If they’ve been undocumented for more than
a year, they’re barred for 10 years.

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:37.079
So if they want to get lawful status through
a job, they’d have to first leave the US,

00:03:37.079 --> 00:03:38.760
for 10 years.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.769
Or through their brother?

00:03:39.769 --> 00:03:41.909
Leave, for 10 years.

00:03:41.909 --> 00:03:43.279
Or through their spouse?

00:03:43.279 --> 00:03:45.537
Leave, for 10 years.

00:03:45.537 --> 00:03:48.419
It's family separation by another name.

00:03:48.419 --> 00:03:53.709
The bars were intended to try to essentially
create punishments

00:03:53.709 --> 00:03:58.029
that were so severe to deter people essentially
from coming here,

00:03:58.029 --> 00:04:04.644
but as we’ve seen with many deterrent-based
policies the practical effect is very different.

00:04:04.644 --> 00:04:09.634
Instead, it incentivized people to stay in
the US undocumented.

00:04:09.634 --> 00:04:13.139
Before IIRIRA, Mexican immigrants who came
to the US unlawfully

00:04:13.139 --> 00:04:16.720
were about 50% likely to return to Mexico
within a year.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:22.400
But after 1996, more people started staying
in the US.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.910
There were around 5 million undocumented immigrants
living in the US before IIRIRA.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:29.699
Today, it’s at least double that.

00:04:29.699 --> 00:04:32.521
And we are somehow surprised by that outcome.

00:04:32.521 --> 00:04:34.751
This is of our own doing.

00:04:34.751 --> 00:04:40.902
Laws like IIRIRA shaped the way the US focuses
on immigration enforcement as a deterrent.

00:04:40.902 --> 00:04:46.721
But really it proved that stronger enforcement
doesn’t actually stop undocumented immigration.

00:04:46.721 --> 00:04:50.350
These laws, and the politics in the 90s, 
didn't really change 

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:53.778
the reasons why people come to the United States,

00:04:53.778 --> 00:04:57.750
Today, views on immigrants are very different
than they were in the 1990s:

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:01.949
most Americans now see them as a strength,
not a burden.

00:05:01.949 --> 00:05:04.879
But the laws created here,
haven’t changed.

00:05:04.879 --> 00:05:10.050
Requirements and standards that were created
decades ago that aren’t responsive

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:15.791
to our needs as a nation and certainly aren’t
responsive to the needs of immigrant populations.

00:05:15.791 --> 00:05:19.681
The idea that, if we only had more guns,
if we only built a higher wall, 

00:05:19.681 --> 00:05:21.000
that would solve all the problems.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.806
I think we learned from 1996 that's not the way it works. 
It's not that simple.

